# Line endings are LF everywhere, decided in the repository rather than left to
# each contributor's core.autocrlf (#212).
#
# Why this exists: nine files carried CRLF in the index — adigator.m plus eight
# under lib/ and examples/ — with nothing declaring or protecting them. Any tool
# that rewrote one of those wholesale with LF turned a small change into a
# full-file diff (a 60-line change once appeared as ~3,100 lines), and under
# squash-merge that collapses `git blame` for the whole file onto one unrelated
# commit. It was also awkward to undo: with core.autocrlf=input, `git add`
# normalises CRLF→LF into the index regardless of the working tree, so restoring
# the endings needed a hash-object/update-index incantation that lived only in
# whoever remembered it.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` ends that class of problem: git normalises to LF in the
# index and checks out LF on every platform, so no tool can reintroduce the
# churn and no contributor needs to know the recipe.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit rather than merely implied by the rule above: these must be LF or
# they fail to execute under sh — a CRLF shebang is not a valid interpreter
# path. Kept so the requirement survives an edit to the line above. (#82)
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary artefacts: never normalise, never diff as text. `text=auto` already
# detects these correctly today, but stating them means a mis-detection cannot
# corrupt a PDF or an image by "normalising" it.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
# .mat especially: the tool GENERATES these routinely in 'c'/'l' modes, so a
# committed fixture is plausible and a mis-detection would corrupt it silently.
*.mat binary
*.mex* binary
